          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My cholesterol came back at 6.4.",
          "answer": "Your total cholesterol result was 6.4, but the guidance I searched does not say whether that number is high for you. Please ask the clinician who ordered the test what it means.\n\nThe main guidance I found is a major cardiology society summary, \"Lipids: screening intervals, risk stratification and statin initiation.\" It says the total cholesterol number is part of a standard lipid panel, but that treatment decisions depend mainly on your estimated 10-year risk of heart and blood vessel disease, not on one cholesterol number alone. It also says a non-fasting sample is acceptable for initial screening in most adults.\n\nThe passages did not give a total cholesterol threshold, did not say which units the 6.4 is in, and did not cover what to do next. They also did not say how your overall risk is calculated for you. Useful questions for your clinician include:\n\n- Is 6.4 high for me, given my age, blood pressure, smoking status, family history and other health factors?\n- What is my estimated 10-year risk?\n- Do I need a repeat test, a fasting test, or other checks?\n- What options should we weigh, and what are the benefits and risks of each?\n\nIf you develop chest pain, pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back, sudden shortness of breath, fainting, or sudden weakness or trouble speaking, call your local emergency number right away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
