          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Does that need treating?",
          "answer": "I can't tell you whether 6.4 needs treatment. The guidance I found does not decide treatment from total cholesterol alone.\n\nThe lipid guidance, \"Lipids: screening intervals, risk stratification and statin initiation,\" says treatment decisions are driven mainly by your estimated 10-year risk of heart and blood vessel disease. That estimate uses your age, sex, total and HDL cholesterol, blood pressure, blood pressure treatment, diabetes, and smoking status. For adults aged 40 to 75 without existing heart disease, it places people into four bands: low (below 5%), borderline (5% to below 7.5%), intermediate (7.5% to below 20%), and high (20% or above). Factors such as family history of early heart disease or chronic kidney disease can shift a decision within the borderline and intermediate bands.\n\nThe passages did not say whether 6.4 is high, which units it uses, or what treatment would be appropriate at any level. They also did not cover your own risk band. Your clinician can calculate that band from your full results and history. Ask them what your 10-year risk is and whether any treatment decision depends on it.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
